<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-10">
  <title>Week 10: Heat and wave equations </title>


<subsection xml:id="w10-heat">
<title>The Heat Equation</title>

<definition>
<title>One-Dimensional Heat Equation</title>
<statement>
<md>u_t=ku_{xx}</md>
<p>Here <m>u(x,t)</m> is temperature and <m>k</m> is the thermal diffusivity.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<table>
<title>Interpretation of the Heat Equation</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Term</cell><cell>Meaning</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_t</m></cell><cell>Rate of temperature change</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>Curvature of temperature profile</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>k</m></cell><cell>Thermal diffusivity</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-separation">
<title>Separation of Variables</title>

<definition>
<title>Separated Solution</title>
<statement>
<md>u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)</md>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Substituting into the heat equation yields</p>
<md>XT'=kX''T</md>
<md>\frac{T'}{kT}=\frac{X''}{X}=-\lambda</md>

<p>This produces two ordinary differential equations.</p>
<md>X''+\lambda X=0</md>
<md>T'+k\lambda T=0</md>

<image width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[-&gt;] (0,0)--(6,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[-&gt;] (0,0)--(0,3) node[above]{$u$};

\draw[blue,thick]
plot[smooth]
coordinates{
(0,0)
(1,.9)
(2,1.5)
(3,1.8)
(4,1.5)
(5,.9)
(6,0)
};

\draw[red,dashed,thick]
plot[smooth]
coordinates{
(0,0)
(1,.5)
(2,.8)
(3,1)
(4,.8)
(5,.5)
(6,0)
};

\node[blue] at (4.5,2.1) {$t=0$};
\node[red] at (4.5,1.1) {$t&gt;0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="w10-heat-example">
<title>Heat Equation on a Finite Rod</title>

<example>
<title>Heat Equation with Zero Boundary Conditions</title>
<statement>
<md>u_t=ku_{xx}, \qquad 0&lt;x&lt;L</md>
<md>u(0,t)=0, \qquad u(L,t)=0</md>
<md>u(x,0)=f(x)</md>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>The spatial problem becomes</p>
<md>X''+\lambda X=0</md>
<md>X(0)=0, \qquad X(L)=0</md>

<p>The eigenfunctions are</p>
<md>X_n(x)=\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)</md>

<p>The time factors are</p>
<md>T_n(t)=e^{-kn^2\pi^2t/L^2}</md>

<p>The solution is</p>
<md>
u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n
\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
e^{-kn^2\pi^2t/L^2}
</md>
</solution>
</example>

<remark>
<title>Interpretation</title>
<p>Higher-frequency modes decay faster, causing temperature distributions to become smoother over time.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-wave">
<title>The Wave Equation</title>

<definition>
<title>One-Dimensional Wave Equation</title>
<statement>
<md>u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}</md>
<p>Here <m>u(x,t)</m> is displacement and <m>c</m> is the wave speed.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<table>
<title>Interpretation of the Wave Equation</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Term</cell><cell>Meaning</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{tt}</m></cell><cell>Acceleration</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>u_{xx}</m></cell><cell>Curvature</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>c</m></cell><cell>Wave speed</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-wave-example">
<title>Vibrating String Example</title>
<image width="50%">
    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[-&gt;] (0,0)--(6.5,0);

\draw[thick,domain=0:6,samples=100]
plot (\x,{1.2*sin(180*\x/6)});

\fill (0,0) circle(2pt);
\fill (6,0) circle(2pt);

\node[below] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[below] at (6,0) {$L$};
\end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>
<example>
<title>String Fixed at Both Ends</title>
<statement>
<md>u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}</md>
<md>u(0,t)=0,\qquad u(L,t)=0</md>
<md>u(x,0)=f(x),\qquad u_t(x,0)=g(x)</md>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>Separation of variables again yields</p>
<md>X_n(x)=\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)</md>

<p>The time-dependent functions satisfy</p>
<md>T''+c^2\lambda T=0</md>

<p>The general solution is</p>
<md>
u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\left[A_n\cos\left(\frac{n\pi ct}{L}\right)
+B_n\sin\left(\frac{n\pi ct}{L}\right)\right]
\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
</md>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-comparison">
<title>Comparing the Heat and Wave Equations</title>

<table>
<title>Heat versus Wave Equation</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Feature</cell><cell>Heat Equation</cell><cell>Wave Equation</cell></row>
<row><cell>PDE Type</cell><cell>Parabolic</cell><cell>Hyperbolic</cell></row>
<row><cell>Process</cell><cell>Diffusion</cell><cell>Propagation</cell></row>
<row><cell>Time Derivative</cell><cell>First Order</cell><cell>Second Order</cell></row>
<row><cell>Long-Term Behavior</cell><cell>Approaches equilibrium</cell><cell>Continues oscillating</cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-matlab">
<title>MATLAB Visualization</title>

<program language="matlab">
L = 1;
x = linspace(0,L,100);
t = linspace(0,0.5,100);
[X,T] = meshgrid(x,t);
U = exp(-pi^2*T).*sin(pi*X);
surf(X,T,U)
xlabel('Position')
ylabel('Time')
zlabel('Temperature')
title('Heat Equation Solution')
</program>

<program language="matlab">
x = linspace(0,1,200);
for t = 0:0.02:4
    u = sin(pi*x).*cos(pi*t);
    plot(x,u,'LineWidth',2)
    axis([0 1 -1.1 1.1])
    drawnow
end
</program>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-fd">
<title>Finite Difference Methods</title>

<definition>
<title>Finite Difference Approximation</title>
<statement>
<md>u_t\approx\frac{u_i^{n+1}-u_i^n}{\Delta t}</md>
<md>u_{xx}\approx\frac{u_{i+1}^n-2u_i^n+u_{i-1}^n}{(\Delta x)^2}</md>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For the heat equation, the explicit finite-difference scheme is</p>

<md>
u_i^{n+1}=u_i^n+r(u_{i+1}^n-2u_i^n+u_{i-1}^n)
</md>

<md>
r=\frac{k\Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2}
</md>

<remark>
<title>Stability Condition</title>
<md>r\le\frac12</md>
</remark>

<program language="matlab">
L = 1;
dx = 0.1;
dt = 0.005;
r = dt/dx^2;
</program>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="w10-error">
<title>Numerical versus Exact Solutions</title>

<p>For</p>
<md>u_t=u_{xx},\qquad u(x,0)=\sin(\pi x)</md>

<p>The exact solution is</p>
<md>u(x,t)=e^{-\pi^2 t}\sin(\pi x)</md>

<table>
<title>Error Measures</title>
<tabular>
<row><cell>Measure</cell><cell>Formula</cell></row>
<row><cell>Absolute Error</cell><cell><m>|u-u_h|</m></cell></row>
<row><cell>Relative Error</cell><cell><m>|u-u_h|/|u|</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>

<activity>
<title>Grid Refinement</title>
<ol>
<li>Compute a finite-difference solution.</li>
<li>Compare with the exact solution.</li>
<li>Reduce <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta t</m>.</li>
<li>Observe how the error changes.</li>
</ol>
</activity>
</subsection>

<summary>
<p>The heat equation models diffusion, while the wave equation models propagation. Separation of variables and Fourier series provide analytical solutions, while finite-difference methods provide numerical approximations when exact solutions are unavailable.</p>
</summary>
</section>